1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,869
Forty hungry students walk past Laddoo
Lab's stall at the college gate.

2
00:00:05,169 --> 00:00:06,972
Only five of them buy a pack.

3
00:00:07,322 --> 00:00:08,222
Why?

4
00:00:07,811 --> 00:00:12,164
Being hungry is not the same as
wanting a laddoo, or being able to pay

5
00:00:12,164 --> 00:00:13,064
for one.

6
00:00:13,501 --> 00:00:15,841
Marketers use three words for this.

7
00:00:15,841 --> 00:00:17,666
Needs, wants and demands.

8
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:20,966
Get them right, and you know
exactly whom to cook for.

9
00:00:21,566 --> 00:00:23,143
So what is the difference?

10
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:28,141
Kotler and Armstrong define all three
in their book, Principles of Marketing.

11
00:00:28,441 --> 00:00:30,930
A need is a state of felt deprivation.

12
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,692
Something basic you
are missing, like food.

13
00:00:33,992 --> 00:00:38,577
A want is the form a need takes,
shaped by your culture and personality.

14
00:00:38,877 --> 00:00:41,489
A demand is a want backed by buying power.

15
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:43,920
You want it, and you can pay for it.

16
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:45,420
Take Mrs.

17
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:46,240
Rao.

18
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:48,211
Her daughter gets hungry at school.

19
00:00:48,211 --> 00:00:49,451
That is a need.

20
00:00:49,751 --> 00:00:52,824
In Bengaluru, the child
wants a sweet laddoo.

21
00:00:52,824 --> 00:00:55,548
A child in London might want a sandwich.

22
00:00:55,848 --> 00:00:57,529
Same need, different wants.

23
00:00:57,829 --> 00:00:58,729
Mrs.

24
00:00:58,318 --> 00:01:02,623
Rao has a hundred and twenty rupees,
and she is happy to spend it.

25
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:04,407
Now the want is a demand.

26
00:01:05,007 --> 00:01:06,803
Marketers don't create needs.

27
00:01:06,803 --> 00:01:09,142
They shape wants, and serve demand.

28
00:01:09,842 --> 00:01:13,008
Now let's count, at the
college gate at four o'clock.

29
00:01:13,308 --> 00:01:15,481
All forty students are hungry.

30
00:01:15,481 --> 00:01:16,509
Forty needs.

31
00:01:16,809 --> 00:01:19,503
Twelve want a healthy
sweet, like a laddoo.

32
00:01:19,503 --> 00:01:21,275
The rest want chips or tea.

33
00:01:21,575 --> 00:01:25,036
But only five can pay a hundred
and twenty rupees today.

34
00:01:25,336 --> 00:01:27,294
So the real demand is five.

35
00:01:27,294 --> 00:01:29,552
Nani cooks for five, not forty.

36
00:01:30,152 --> 00:01:32,499
Needs also come in levels.

37
00:01:32,499 --> 00:01:36,533
In 1943, Abraham Maslow
described five of them.

38
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:39,651
First, body needs, like food and sleep.

39
00:01:39,651 --> 00:01:40,620
Then safety.

40
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,585
Then love and belonging.

41
00:01:42,585 --> 00:01:45,403
Then esteem, which
means feeling respected.

42
00:01:45,703 --> 00:01:48,034
And at the top, self actualization.

43
00:01:48,034 --> 00:01:49,636
Becoming your best self.

44
00:01:49,936 --> 00:01:52,414
One laddoo can meet more than one level.

45
00:01:52,714 --> 00:01:54,444
It fills a hungry child.

46
00:01:54,444 --> 00:01:55,462
And for Mrs.

47
00:01:55,462 --> 00:01:58,723
Rao, a healthy tiffin is
a way of showing love.

48
00:01:59,323 --> 00:02:01,290
Where do we see this at work today?

49
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:02,490
Needs.

50
00:02:02,254 --> 00:02:05,510
A hospital meets the
basic need to get well.

51
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:06,710
Wants.

52
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:10,639
A phone maker sells one phone in
many colours, for different tastes.

53
00:02:10,939 --> 00:02:11,839
Demand.

54
00:02:11,556 --> 00:02:15,798
An airline checks how many people
will really pay, before it adds a new

55
00:02:15,798 --> 00:02:16,698
route.

56
00:02:16,968 --> 00:02:18,992
But be careful with these ideas.

57
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:20,339
The good side.

58
00:02:20,339 --> 00:02:23,386
Knowing the real need helps
you serve people well.

59
00:02:23,686 --> 00:02:24,779
The hard side.

60
00:02:24,779 --> 00:02:29,527
Ads can make a want feel like a need,
and push people to spend too much.

61
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:32,845
Arjun wants to let students
eat now and pay later.

62
00:02:33,145 --> 00:02:34,246
Zoya says no.

63
00:02:34,246 --> 00:02:36,599
Without money, it is not demand.

64
00:02:36,599 --> 00:02:37,598
It is debt.

65
00:02:37,898 --> 00:02:40,082
And Maslow's famous pyramid?

66
00:02:40,082 --> 00:02:43,749
A 2019 study found that
Maslow never drew one.

67
00:02:44,049 --> 00:02:44,949
The lesson?

68
00:02:44,908 --> 00:02:47,400
Serve real needs, and never fake them.

69
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:48,936
Quick check.

70
00:02:48,936 --> 00:02:52,110
A student loves laddoos,
but has no money today.

71
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:53,596
Which word fits best?

72
00:02:55,496 --> 00:02:56,396
Want!

73
00:02:55,996 --> 00:03:00,045
He wants the laddoo, but without
buying power, it is not a demand.

74
00:03:00,645 --> 00:03:01,658
So, remember.

75
00:03:01,958 --> 00:03:05,018
A need is something basic
you are missing, like food.

76
00:03:05,368 --> 00:03:09,746
A want is the form a need takes,
shaped by culture and personality.

77
00:03:10,096 --> 00:03:12,886
A demand is a want backed by buying power.

78
00:03:13,486 --> 00:03:17,070
Next time, we'll explore
customer value and satisfaction.
